- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290ee5b8-cbf7-5232-b509-85b70d336edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238419} {:answer/id #uuid "5d98a1c9-ea74-5d5a-9235-9cb7b2ccd0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238871} {:answer/id #uuid "939ad9f4-b5dd-56c0-9292-76f77d15c465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239330} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e613a5-28c7-5ab0-88dd-d53e6c4d9408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239739} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c8f4ee-acc5-5ce3-8962-6b1e968a9d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "290ee5b8-cbf7-5232-b509-85b70d336edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238419} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe985a1-5ce0-54a3-83a9-d5b67f4adb67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e62d29f-2834-5b4e-b720-bc361e2a5dc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104702" :question/problem "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Z}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-62$\n- $0$\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $-\\frac{56}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative integer numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-62$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{56}{4}=-14$ belongs to ${Z}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f508bc1-5c8c-5dac-b648-e683e6948a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238320} {:answer/id #uuid "97344af5-4d0b-50ac-8b3e-e8c06cd07b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239364} {:answer/id #uuid "aca5e5b6-04b0-5494-8936-3a1199e5007a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239555} {:answer/id #uuid "b65deb58-d812-5ef0-9eed-c2321a16ab5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239648} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32c1df-bb7d-58a9-84d1-726cffbc66e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97344af5-4d0b-50ac-8b3e-e8c06cd07b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239364} :answer-field/id #uuid "b72163d1-46c0-5c21-9c83-5ecf0b5030da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1da25f7d-182b-5d2d-b5c8-9d3f66bb445c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104703" :question/problem "To which of the following sets does the number $\\sqrt{121}$ belong?\n\n- $N$\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- ${Z}^{-}$\n- $Z$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $\\sqrt{121}=11$ is a natural number, so it belongs to $N.$\n\nSince every natural number is also an integer number, $\\sqrt{121}$ also belongs to $Z.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the sets ${Z}^{-}$ and ${Q}^{-}$ only contain negative numbers. Therefore, $\\sqrt{121}$ does not belong to either of these sets. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfa0d1d-6451-5387-be02-629f948a0f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238176} {:answer/id #uuid "11413563-1bf2-5444-8474-5e5cf301d8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238195} {:answer/id #uuid "19f29985-57b5-5076-80ec-5cd7f22a238f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238269} {:answer/id #uuid "97d4f97f-b63c-51b3-ad86-5cecad9eefc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239370} {:answer/id #uuid "ebab29aa-9a93-58e7-aefd-391feb8f233c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebab29aa-9a93-58e7-aefd-391feb8f233c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240129} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac216de7-0aa2-506b-ba09-2a8b5ef341b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95a9cd65-803f-5d19-875e-716631b2f8a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104734" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d3d4890-6f13-5dde-8095-3e3fced3ec6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238867} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7da710-4947-5407-a417-4e130b1e610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239007} {:answer/id #uuid "771614d9-5d43-5f3d-ad3a-5323c7edc76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239081} {:answer/id #uuid "aa902358-41c8-51c0-b7ed-68bafa845a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239547} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a18bf8-4f0e-5c76-ac9a-9b37b5796acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3a18bf8-4f0e-5c76-ac9a-9b37b5796acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239913} :answer-field/id #uuid "873915d1-3cba-59f7-b3bd-b2e37c5ee6c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d2cd958-2383-51a8-8ef7-f3c1ff9a5b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-104736" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6ab324-e5d5-54bc-81f0-75fea5bb330d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238311} {:answer/id #uuid "3a08ac4a-4091-58c6-92af-1125cbea741c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238559} {:answer/id #uuid "3acac4f5-4282-5572-8280-d6cef3c271a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238565} {:answer/id #uuid "a162af6d-c1e0-55bb-8110-b150da1039b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239459} {:answer/id #uuid "c80ddecd-e80b-5833-b146-c2c88d4a543a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a162af6d-c1e0-55bb-8110-b150da1039b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239459} :answer-field/id #uuid "c284c296-c6a6-52f7-9741-e3dc22502ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f571336-e197-53b2-942f-7691710c5fd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104778" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${0,1}?$\n\n- $p:{0,1}\\to Z$\n- $q:[0,1]\\to {0,1}$\n- $r:{0,1}\\to (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is ${0,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(0,1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (excluding both $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444c9197-bf9a-595e-9928-a00d60bf51ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238646} {:answer/id #uuid "48ab53f8-8b1f-598c-b301-1fb5d97fd076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238690} {:answer/id #uuid "98c80c08-b713-5407-b264-97db66af82b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239381} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f96900-f753-561b-97e8-5b406d478b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239939} {:answer/id #uuid "daa8044c-f7da-5f1d-b8f2-e0a8751f5b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7f96900-f753-561b-97e8-5b406d478b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239939} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae58ed1-7247-5583-af27-e33f0f49a422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00f5a8c6-457a-5b26-87c0-0c588245cf9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104780" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $Z?$\n\n- $p:Z\\to [0,3]$\n- $q:(0,7)\\to Z$\n- $r:{Z}^{+}\\to Z$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(0,7),$ the interval from $0$ to $7$ (excluding $0$ and $7$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is ${Z}^{+},$ the set of positive integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e11ce7-772d-5167-aacc-c50653d1da8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238252} {:answer/id #uuid "669e1527-0fe0-5c06-b32f-423993073528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238950} {:answer/id #uuid "8f15fd0f-7424-5e92-b313-a15ec5b93ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239301} {:answer/id #uuid "af228750-6768-51c1-8675-12a42677d2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239578} {:answer/id #uuid "c593d50f-219d-5028-9ad4-960392042586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f15fd0f-7424-5e92-b313-a15ec5b93ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239301} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1c8172-8f3d-5164-b238-a3a89a22c624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3958191-a06f-54c9-9b69-6e7038fa786f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104781" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $p:[-2,2]\\to (-2,2)$\n- $r:[-2,2]\\to {-2,2}$\n- $q:Z\\to [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $[-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ and $2$).\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $[-2,2],$ the interval from $-2$ to $2$ (including $-2$ and $2$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6320076d-f184-5505-9afa-066450dc7e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238925} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8f13b3-1fbe-5065-bdb6-0ac9afedb7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239018} {:answer/id #uuid "748dd741-9dc6-51eb-b44f-5d4106157729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239058} {:answer/id #uuid "7c25f0ee-4977-5144-9f98-99d8e7619ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239126} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6ef1b9-ca5d-5247-86e0-65ac9418fa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c25f0ee-4977-5144-9f98-99d8e7619ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239126} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5dfd15-d15e-51e0-842d-fdbae1516db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4740436b-fb84-571a-aea1-e76558938555" :question/math-academy-id "q-104916" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{12},{20,22},{6,9,15}}?$\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$\n- ${{20,22}}$\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${-1,{12}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $-1\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{20,22}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{0},{6,9,15}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${0}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c942801-4233-56d7-9fb8-ceb97d123362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238294} {:answer/id #uuid "68461084-a8de-521f-b799-1dd5709cfda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238961} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e5a170-5b45-500a-8676-391714d56859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239829} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8ed2f5-5559-580e-8304-67e8460a2ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239865} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a916a1-8aee-5f26-8f14-99f490d68e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e5a170-5b45-500a-8676-391714d56859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239829} :answer-field/id #uuid "4680294b-b316-53ae-9fc5-7faee29b649b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "caad9560-3559-5568-b085-de268d2408da" :question/math-academy-id "q-104945" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:{0,1}\\to Z?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $Z$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:{0,1}\\to Z\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set ${0,1}$ to the set $Z.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is ${0,1},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Z.$ Note that ${0,1}⊆Z$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170f27a7-adab-5fb3-a43b-22015f9de6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238254} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6b9b0b-0581-587e-a19e-5c61236e4e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238460} {:answer/id #uuid "47044167-e5aa-5119-a140-0e4d7ee5f0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238672} {:answer/id #uuid "bca9a8b0-a6cc-57c6-aeb9-b630a482addb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239700} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7bda4d-9283-5415-a3d1-907a039a7cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca9a8b0-a6cc-57c6-aeb9-b630a482addb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239700} :answer-field/id #uuid "200710a7-fe7f-5bb1-bea5-13c8f8caa4aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ece458da-55a3-56bf-9d0d-49bed0ba9d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104946" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:Q\\to R?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $R$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $R$\n- The range of $g$ is $Q$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:Q\\to R\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $Q$ to the set $R.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $R.$ Note that $Q⊂R$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23902f14-27f2-5101-84f0-7c1806396a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238368} {:answer/id #uuid "2b359311-6c75-5aba-a57a-fe93b771abf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238437} {:answer/id #uuid "406d00a9-cdd8-5229-81cd-eff712bd62dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238612} {:answer/id #uuid "b23afc17-039f-5aee-944b-9492730d88c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239611} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cbd10d-9ab8-56b1-b1c4-feec697ddb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b359311-6c75-5aba-a57a-fe93b771abf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238437} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd9cde6-7000-5e6b-b8ab-9e58d2ac529a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86293bdd-1445-5c75-b0fe-3a52f74f9647" :question/math-academy-id "q-104954" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0484eaf7-9412-5914-9529-5a91fc451c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238119} {:answer/id #uuid "24e2ea54-d24f-52e0-90d0-2e855dfc1a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238378} {:answer/id #uuid "d52ad722-7dd6-5480-aef6-d03d8ba2f94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239925} {:answer/id #uuid "dbedb9fd-99da-55fb-ad10-e905d50c863f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239974} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bebc67-3e94-59dd-997c-7ad86745d333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7bebc67-3e94-59dd-997c-7ad86745d333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240246} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3801784-8479-5814-8073-11bcaba8056c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63db395b-88f4-58c0-8886-5830de0a85c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105033" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive rational numbers)?\n\nI. $Z$\n\nII. ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n\nIII. $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in Z$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ Every negative integer is a non-positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20da86c9-b413-5fd3-b966-bced38d2961e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238341} {:answer/id #uuid "57e08709-9551-574f-8693-ace404a47411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238811} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe6aaa-8ca3-51c2-a5a2-bbfdb146ee36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239596} {:answer/id #uuid "bd808711-0908-53fc-aae9-25e26770bdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239708} {:answer/id #uuid "e1104fa6-ef2f-5563-a8e6-2114658991b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57e08709-9551-574f-8693-ace404a47411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238811} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5dcdb8f-b293-5d92-a703-bf52db6e296e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb139d93-059f-53af-a8f9-6a747e8ff56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105034" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ (the set of negative rationals)\n- ${Z}^{+}$ (the set of positive integers)\n- $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every negative rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{+}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every positive integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."}]}
